::ALG2_U06_L02_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence is arithmetic?
{
=A. 6, 10, 14, 18#Correct. The common difference is 4.
~B. 6, 12, 24, 48#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 2.
~C. 6, 9, 15, 24#The differences are not constant.
~D. 6, 18, 54, 162#This is geometric because each term is multiplied by 3.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence is geometric?
{
~A. 5, 9, 13, 17#This is arithmetic with common difference 4.
=B. 5, 15, 45, 135#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 3.
~C. 5, 8, 12, 17#The differences are not constant and there is no common ratio.
~D. 5, 10, 15, 20#This is arithmetic with common difference 5.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the common difference of 12, 9, 6, 3, ...?
{
~A. 3#The terms decrease, so the difference is negative.
~B. 12#This is the first term, not the difference.
=C. -3#Correct. Each term changes by -3.
~D. -9#This is not the repeated change between consecutive terms.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the common ratio of 4, 12, 36, 108, ...?
{
~A. 4#This is the first term, not the ratio.
~B. 8#This is the first difference, not a repeated multiplier.
~C. 12#This is the second term, not the ratio.
=D. 3#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 3.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the 8th term of 2, 5, 8, 11, ...?
{
=A. 23#Correct. The common difference is 3, so a_8\=2+7(3)\=23.
~B. 20#This is the 7th term.
~C. 26#This adds one extra common difference.
~D. 16#This stops too early in the sequence.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q06
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the 5th term of 3, 6, 12, 24, ...?
{
~A. 30#This adds a constant difference instead of multiplying.
=B. 48#Correct. The common ratio is 2, so the 5th term is 48.
~C. 36#This assumes a constant difference of 9.
~D. 96#This is the 6th term, not the 5th.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q07
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which explicit rule matches an arithmetic sequence with a_1\=4 and d\=6?
{
~A. a_n\=4(6)ⁿ⁻¹#This is a geometric rule, not arithmetic.
~B. a_n\=6+4(n-1)#This swaps the first term and common difference.
=C. a_n\=4+6(n-1)#Correct. Arithmetic sequences use a_1+(n-1)d.
~D. a_n\=4+6n#This starts one common difference too high.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q08
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which explicit rule matches a geometric sequence with a_1\=2 and r\=5?
{
~A. a_n\=2+5(n-1)#This is arithmetic, not geometric.
~B. a_n\=5(2)ⁿ⁻¹#This swaps the first term and ratio.
~C. a_n\=2(5)ⁿ#This starts one exponent too high.
=D. a_n\=2(5)ⁿ⁻¹#Correct. Geometric sequences use a_1rⁿ⁻¹.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q09
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What type of model is connected to an arithmetic sequence?
{
=A. linear#Correct. A constant difference corresponds to a constant rate of change.
~B. exponential#Exponential models connect to constant ratios.
~C. quadratic#A quadratic pattern does not have a constant first difference.
~D. reciprocal#A reciprocal model does not describe a constant difference pattern.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q10
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What type of model is connected to a geometric sequence?
{
~A. linear#Linear models connect to constant differences.
=B. exponential#Correct. A constant ratio corresponds to exponential behavior.
~C. absolute value#Absolute value graphs do not represent repeated multiplication.
~D. constant#A geometric sequence changes by a repeated multiplier, not necessarily stays fixed.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q11
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the sum of the first 4 terms of 2, 5, 8, 11, ...?
{
~A. 11#This is only the 4th term.
~B. 20#This omits one term from the sum.
=C. 26#Correct. 2+5+8+11\=26.
~D. 32#This includes an extra term.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q12
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the sum of the first 3 terms of 4, 8, 16, ...?
{
~A. 16#This is only the third term.
~B. 20#This omits the second term.
~C. 24#This adds the first two terms twice.
=D. 28#Correct. 4+8+16\=28.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q13
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which recursive rule matches 7, 10, 13, 16, ...?
{
=A. a_1\=7; a_n\=a_n-1+3#Correct. Start at 7 and add 3 each time.
~B. a_1\=7; a_n\=3a_n-1#This multiplies by 3 and would be geometric.
~C. a_1\=3; a_n\=a_n-1+7#This swaps the starting term and common difference.
~D. a_n\=7+3n#This is explicit-style notation and starts too high for n\=1.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q14
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which recursive rule matches 2, 8, 32, 128, ...?
{
~A. a_1\=2; a_n\=a_n-1+4#This creates an arithmetic sequence.
=B. a_1\=2; a_n\=4a_n-1#Correct. Start at 2 and multiply by 4 each time.
~C. a_1\=4; a_n\=2a_n-1#This swaps the starting term and ratio.
~D. a_n\=2+4(n-1)#This is an arithmetic explicit rule.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q15
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence has common ratio 1/2?
{
~A. 2, 4, 8, 16#This has common ratio 2.
~B. 2, 3, 4, 5#This has common difference 1.
=C. 16, 8, 4, 2#Correct. Each term is multiplied by 1/2.
~D. 16, 12, 8, 4#This has common difference -4.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q16
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which sequence has common difference -5?
{
~A. 5, 10, 15, 20#This has common difference 5.
~B. 40, 20, 10, 5#This is geometric with ratio 1/2.
~C. 3, -3, -9, -15#This has common difference -6.
=D. 20, 15, 10, 5#Correct. Each term decreases by 5.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q17
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is a series?
{
=A. the sum of terms in a sequence#Correct. A series adds sequence terms together.
~B. a single term in a sequence#A term is one value, not a sum.
~C. the ratio between terms#A ratio helps identify geometric sequences.
~D. the difference between terms#A difference helps identify arithmetic sequences.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q18
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the 4th term of a_n\=3+2(n-1)?
{
~A. 7#This is the 3rd term.
=B. 9#Correct. a_4\=3+2(3)\=9.
~C. 11#This adds one extra common difference.
~D. 12#This multiplies instead of using the arithmetic rule.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q19
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the 4th term of a_n\=3(2)ⁿ⁻¹?
{
~A. 12#This uses repeated addition instead of multiplication.
~B. 18#This uses one fewer factor of 2.
=C. 24#Correct. a_4\=3(2)³\=24.
~D. 48#This uses one extra factor of 2.
}


::ALG2_U06_L02_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L02_Q20
MLA Standard: Support-only readiness; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which pattern is neither arithmetic nor geometric?
{
~A. 1, 4, 7, 10#This is arithmetic with common difference 3.
~B. 2, 6, 18, 54#This is geometric with common ratio 3.
~C. 10, 5, 0, -5#This is arithmetic with common difference -5.
=D. 1, 2, 4, 7#Correct. The differences and ratios are not constant.
}

